---
title: Pancakes
description: Thick, fluffy pancakes from a lumpy batter rested 10 minutes.
tags: [pancakes, griddle]
course: breakfast
icons: [pancakes, butter, egg]
difficulty: easy
source:
  name: Cook's Illustrated
  url: https://www.americastestkitchen.com/recipes/10906-easy-pancakes
servings: 4
yield: 16%pancakes
time: 45m
---

Whisk the @all-purpose flour{283%g}(10 oz, 2 cups), @sugar{3%tbsp}, @baking powder{4%tsp}, @baking soda{1/2%tsp}, and @salt{1%tsp} together in a #large bowl{}.

Whisk the @large eggs{2} and @vegetable oil{1/4%cup} in a second #medium bowl{} until well combined. Whisk the @milk{1 1/2%cup} and @vanilla extract{1/2%tsp} into the egg mixture.

Add the egg mixture to the flour mixture and stir gently until just combined (the batter should remain lumpy with a few streaks of flour). Let the batter sit for ~rest{10%minutes} before cooking.

Heat @&vegetable oil{1/2%tsp} in a 12-inch nonstick #skillet{} over medium-low heat until shimmering. Using #paper towels{}, carefully wipe out the oil, leaving a thin film on the bottom and sides of the skillet.

> **On a griddle** — The pancakes can be cooked on an electric griddle set to 350°F (175°C).

Drop 1 tablespoon of batter in the center of the skillet. If the pancake is pale golden brown after 1 minute, the skillet is ready. If it is too light or too dark, adjust the heat accordingly.

Using a 1/4-cup dry #measuring cup{}, portion the batter into the skillet in 3 places, leaving 2 inches between portions. If necessary, gently spread the batter into a 4-inch round. Cook until the edges are set, the first sides are golden brown, and bubbles on the surface are just beginning to break, ~cook{2-3%minutes}.

Using a thin, wide #spatula{}, flip the pancakes and continue to cook until the second sides are golden brown, ~cook{1-2%minutes} longer. Serve.

Repeat with the remaining batter, using the remaining @&vegetable oil{1/2%tsp} as necessary.

> **Keeping warm** — They can be held in a preheated 200°F (95°C) oven on a wire rack set in a rimmed baking sheet.

> **Serving** — Serve with salted butter and maple syrup, or with a flavored butter: ginger-molasses, orange-almond, or pumpkin spice.

> **Add-ins** — Don't stir fruit, chocolate chips, or nuts directly into the batter. This will overmix it, compromising the pancakes' height and texture. Instead, add them (cut into 1/2-inch pieces) immediately after portioning the batter into the skillet. The batter will partially surround the add-ins during cooking.

> **Storing** — Put cooled pancakes in a zipper-lock bag with waxed paper, parchment paper, or aluminum foil between them. Press the air out before sealing. Refrigerate for up to 3 days or freeze for up to 2 weeks.

> **Reheating** — Heat the pancakes on a rimmed baking sheet in a 325°F (165°C) oven or toaster oven until warmed through: 3 to 4 minutes if refrigerated, about 6 minutes if frozen.
